description.narrative:
- In consideration of the new labour code of 2019 and international labour standards, the Prime Minister issued a Decision 449/QD/Ttg on May 2021 to expand the duties and restructure the National Wage Council (NWC), to strengthen the technical capcity for minimum wage setting by adding two independent experts among its 13 members.
- The Government also took measures to improve the evidence-base for minimum wage setting, by issuing decision # 28 on Feb. 2021 for developing and publishing the first-ever National Wage Report, which will provide   new indicators and methodologies to set adequate minimum wages, taking into account the needs of workers and their families and economic factors in Viet Nam. The evidence-base for minimum wage setting has been further strengthened through the NWC annual report on minimum wage.
- The Government established  a technical working group to carry out an in-depth assessment for the ratification of the ILO convention number 131 on minimum wage;
- The Ministry in charge of Labour (MOLISA) has initiated measures to support the social partners and the National Wage Council towards making stronger use of evidence-based criteria and improved mechanisms to collect and share relevant data to inform collective wage bargaining.
 - ILO provided timely technical and financial support for ensuring the effectiveness of the National Wage Council (NWC) operation through the development of NWC working regulation/mechanism reports and action plan;
- ILO organized the technical seminars to share the good practices and guide the national experts in the development of the National Wage Report;
- ILO provided technical comments for finalization of study on the possible introduction of an hourly minimum wage and provided support for the evidence-based annual report on minimum wage adjustment;
- ILO provided support for Viet Nam Chamber of Commerce and Industry (VCCI) to develop and disseminate information to its members with a brochure on wages, in line with the labour code 2019;
- ILO supported for translation of the global wage report, a brief minimum wage in Viet Nam, and ILO basic information on minimum wage as part of efforts to strengthen the technical capacity on wage policy for critical stakeholders.
- With the support of the Swedish Government, the Ministry in charge of Labour (MOLISA) has initiated measures to develop national guidelines on collective wage bargaining. The Ministry has also fostered discussions for the strengthening of wage bargaining amongst social partners and the National Wage Council based on key findings of two studies commissioned by the ILO. Key findings of those studies include i) the identification of a strong effect of minimum wages on labour productivity where multi-employer collective bargaining agreements exist; and ii) the need to increase the availability of indicators as well as improved collection of relevant data that could inform social partners during wage negotiations.

- In light of the adopted labour code 2019, the Government in consultation with the Social Partners has issued decree # 145 on 14 December 2020 to guide the implementation of various labour technical areas (working conditions, contract, working/resting time, industrial relations, social dialogues, social insurance, etc.), while the issues of Worker Representative Organisations and collective bargaining are still being delayed;
- A work- plan for improving Labour Dispute Resolution system at the national level and three provinces was endorsed by the Department of Industrial Relation and Wage, Ministry of Labour, Invalid and Social Affair (DIRWA, MoLISA) on 25 June 2021 and is under implementation.
- The National Industrial Relation Commission was re-established on July 2021 and lead by a Deputy Prime Minister, with a more effective function and operationalization mechanism that responds to the need of labour code 2019 and industrial relation practical requirements;
- First-ever Prime Minster directive (16/CT-TTg, dated 14 June 2021) was issued for ensuring the decent work, raising living standards, improving working conditions of workers, especially in the crisis context; - ILO provided technical and financial support for Government and Social Partners in the development, consultation and finalization of implementing decree 145;
- For implementation of LC2019 provisions, ILO has made huge efforts in helping MoLISA to develop a implementation plan for improving LDR system at national level and three dynamic provinces including Binh Duong, Dong Nai and HCM city. The work-plan includes activities to strengthen the contingent of mediators and arbitrators and developing operational guidelines for both mechanisms;
- ILO provided technical and financial catalyst support for re-establishing the National Industrial Relation Commission, led by Deputy Prime Minister;
- Under the One UN efforts, ILO provided the significant comments and inputs for mainstreaming the "Decent Work for All" agenda into the draft of the National Social Economic Development Strategy and Social Economic Development Plan , 2021-2030 and vision to 2045,

- In line with the ratified C.98, the collective bargaining practices were upgraded and promoted by Viet Nam General Confederation of Labour (VGCL) and the Provincial Federation of Labour in targeted localities.  These interventions have been made through the Industrial Zone's Trade Union (IZTU) network with specific results such as 5 Multi-Employer Bargaining Agreements signed, benefitting 32,000 workers in 62 enterprises involved; 3 Human Resource Clubs (HR Clubs) established, comprising 131 Human Resource managers in the wood processing sector in Binh Duong and the tourism sector in Da Nang and Quang Nam. The capacity of the HR Clubs has been built on workplace cooperation and on collective bargaining based on active processes of consultation.
- MOLISA has produced the periodic national industrial relations report. The report is a tripartite consensus-based document reflecting both the views of national constituents on what are the challenges in the Viernamese system of industrial relations (with a particular focus on collective bargainign) and a roadmap of possible actions to be taken in order to address such challenges in the future. The report contains sex-disaggregated data and was presented and published throughout the country. - ILO provided the technical and financial support for the Industrial Zone Trade Union's champion network (Trade Union leaders in 50 Industrial zones/parks in 48 cities/provinces) were capacitated for playing a strategic role in promoting trade union reform at grassroots levels; ILO provided the technical support to the legal advisory network through strengthening the capacity of 15 key legal advisors becomes more capable and provides effective supports to workers in various types and services; 
- Through the regular technical meetings supported by the ILO, the 03 sectoral HR Clubs with 131 members in 3 cities/provinces have been playing important roles in building good labour practices and maintaining the good industrial relations;
- For periodical industrial relation reports, ILO supported MoLISA in hiring a team of consultants to work with the Institute of Labour Science and Social Affairs experts for gathering data, conducting analysis, and verifying the findings with tripartite partners, as well as support for publication.
